diff --git a/docs/desktop-heat-20261111.md b/docs/desktop-heat-20261111.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..17638b3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/desktop-heat-20261111.md
@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+# Desktop heat follow-up: November 11 device capture
+
+The supplied logs identify release c17da770d41eef2247a2b928f5a8bfefc51f4edd.
+Kernel read cache and Superblocks are enabled; grouped accesses, WFI spinning,
+profiling, expensive wait probes and GPU timing are disabled. Timer-direct and
+secondary deadline filtering are enabled. This is not a cache/superblocks-off
+comparison, and it cannot establish their causal thermal effect.
+
+Process CPU deltas correspond to3.4–3.8 aggregate host CPU cores in early
+startup intervals and1.1–1.8 in several quieter intervals. Warm is reported39s
+after VM start; Hot about104s. Guest WFI stops after startup, while WFE and
+inter-vCPU acknowledgements continue. Some intervals spend40–52% of one CPU's
+wall interval awaiting TLBI acknowledgement; this is elapsed waiting, not CPU
+consumption. GPU execution timing is disabled, so GPU energy cannot be assigned.
+A4K video surface appears later, after the first Hot report: it cannot explain
+the earlier temperature rise by itself.
+
+Read-span reuse runs at roughly5K–23K/s. About274000 secondary periodic kicks
+are deferred by the140s sample. Retired metadata advice reaches679MiB at the
+first capacity reset. These paths execute, but they have not solved desktop heat.
+The attached hvprof-words.txt contains November10 dumps. Profiling is disabled
+in the current process, so those samples must not be attributed to this release.
+
+## Concrete source repairs
+
+Acknowledgement waits previously slept on ack_seq. A peer becoming parked
+wakes waiters without changing ack_seq. If that notification reaches the kernel
+before the waiter enters the syscall, the waiter can still sleep until the100us
+fallback. A separate32-bit notification generation now changes for both ack and
+park events. Arm/recheck and all real acknowledgement semantics remain intact;
+the same100us missed-notification/own-request-service bound remains.
+A regression injects both notifications exactly after the predicate check and
+before the real kernel wait, with no timing sleep.
+
+Basic WFE elapsed/timeouts now accompany existing interval statistics, independently
+of expensive peer-state probes. Per-vCPU cumulative Mach CPU time is queried only
+when the existing periodic report runs, with lifecycle generation labels and
+explicit unavailable results. Continue replaces the owned Mach thread right
+instead of retaining a dead thread identifier. Reporting and replacement hold
+the existing per-CPU request mutex so a queried port cannot be freed/reused
+mid-sample. Word profiles include PID, and
+disabled profiling explicitly warns that an existing file can be historical.
+
+These repairs improve wait correctness and attribution. They are not evidence
+that WFE spinning, GPU work or the entire desktop thermal problem is fixed.
+No guest timing, rendering quality, CPU count or performance caps are changed.
+No new IPA is requested by this checkpoint.
